      sources: "Sources: examples/privileged-access (tests and fixtures, fictional). Chapter 08, “Lab 1: fixtures, no tenant,” steps 1–4.",
      say: `Lab 1 runs on a laptop with no tenant. First, run Learn's queries over the fictional audit rows. Eight tests pass, and the third shows the trap: two of the rows the first query returns are assignments made outside PIM. Next, list the run's permissions. There are nine, and none of them can write. Then run two days against the fictional tenant. The second day has three Tier 0 findings: a role assigned outside PIM, an activation nobody approved, and a changed role setting. Open the events file, and you'll see IDs, not names.`,
      detail: `You need PowerShell 7.1 or later with Pester 5, Python 3.10 or later with pytest and PyYAML, and a shell with sha256sum (Linux, macOS or Git Bash). Steps 1 to 4 run in examples/privileged-access in PowerShell; steps 5 to 10 in examples/drift-pipeline in a POSIX shell. Step 1, run Learn's queries over the fixture: Invoke-Pester -Path tests/EvidenceDrift.Tests.ps1 -FullNameFilter '*as published*' -Output None -PassThru | Select-Object PassedCount, FailedCount, NotRunCount gives PassedCount 8, FailedCount 0, NotRunCount 24. Read the third test: two of the 13 rows the first query returns are role assignments made outside PIM. Step 2, list the permissions the run needs: Import-Module ./powershell/PaEvidence/PaEvidence.psm1; (Get-PaEvidencePermission -Task evidence-run).GraphPermissions -join ', ' gives AuditLog.Read.All, GroupMember.ReadBasic.All, Policy.Read.All, RoleAssignmentSchedule.Read.Directory, RoleEligibilitySchedule.Read.Directory, RoleManagement.Read.Directory, User.Read.All, User.ReadBasic.All, UserAuthenticationMethod.Read.All. Nothing ends in ReadWrite. Step 3, run two days against the fictional tenant: a $lab splat with Cloud gcc, the fixture tenant ID, OutputPath out/runs, the audit fixture, the emergency configuration, validations and exceptions inputs, three Azure scopes and the control-plane subscription ID; then ./Invoke-PaEvidenceRun.ps1 @lab -FixturePath tests/fixtures/tenant.json -AsOf 2026-10-29T06:00:00Z and ./Invoke-PaEvidenceRun.ps1 @lab -FixturePath tests/fixtures/ch08/tenant-day2.json -AsOf 2026-10-30T06:00:00Z. Each prints three lines; the second run: "Run 2026-10-30T0600Z-fixture (Fixture, gcc): inventory OK, emergency-access OK, pim-audit OK"; "Audit window 2026-10-29T06:00:00Z to 2026-10-30T06:00:00Z"; "Written to <path>/out/runs/2026-10-30T0600Z-fixture (run.json lists each task's manifest hash)". Step 4, read the second day's audit findings: three Tier 0 Findings: PA-AUD-01 for Privileged Role Administrator assigned to …000000000020 outside PIM (two events, one finding), PA-AUD-02 for a Global Administrator activation by …000000000013 with no approval, and PA-AUD-04 for the Global Administrator role setting change. Day 1 has none: both of its activations were approved by someone else. Open events.json: object IDs, no names. Delete out when you're done; the runs in step 5 are the repository's copies of these two, which a Pester test checks against a fresh run. The commands run Learn's pinned queries over fictional audit rows, and the example against a fictional tenant served by a fake. The sample workflow has been parsed and linted, not run.`,
